      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Amplitude and Phase of a Sinusoid",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Identifying Phase with a Negative Offset",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Identify the amplitude and phase of $x(t)=2\\cos(7t-\\pi/4)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match the signal to the standard cosine form $x(t)=A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)$. In this form, the coefficient in front of cosine is the amplitude, and the constant term inside the argument is the phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2\\cos(7t-\\pi/4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the coefficient is $2$, so the amplitude is $A=2$. That is the positive peak deviation, not the angular frequency $7$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(A,\\phi)=(2,-\\pi/4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The constant term inside the cosine argument is $-\\pi/4$, so the phase is $\\phi=-\\pi/4$. The amplitude-phase pair is $(2,-\\pi/4)$, and no rewrite is needed because the task is to read the phase sign directly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Amplitude and Phase of a Sinusoid",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Rewriting a Negative Coefficient with Positive Amplitude",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $x(t)=-4\\cos(3t-\\pi/6)$ in positive-amplitude form and identify its amplitude and phase.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the negative-coefficient cosine form. Use $-\\cos(\\theta)=\\cos(\\theta+\\pi)$ so the sign moves into the phase and the amplitude becomes positive.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=-4\\cos(3t-\\pi/6)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the identity inside the cosine argument and keep the frequency term unchanged. The coefficient becomes $4$, and the phase gains a $\\pi$ shift.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=4\\cos((3t-\\pi/6)+\\pi)=4\\cos(3t+5\\pi/6)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(A,\\phi)=(4,5\\pi/6)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The positive-amplitude standard form is $x(t)=4\\cos(3t+5\\pi/6)$. The amplitude is $A=4$, and the phase is $\\phi=5\\pi/6$ after absorbing the negative sign into the phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
